  "semantic_findings": [
    {
      "topic": "Finite neighborhood subcover",
      "finding": "The selected finite set indexes the subtype K. Each supplied U_x is an ambient neighborhood of x; the Mathlib proof passes to interiors to obtain a finite cover, then proves the union of the original U_x is a neighborhood of all K. The output need not itself be open and does not claim to be open."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Quantifier order",
      "finding": "For each x in K, the local hypothesis chooses U,C,A before all y in U and all orders k. The finite cover and both sum constants are chosen once before all y and k in the conclusion. No order-dependent cover or hidden k-dependent majorant is used."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Targets varying with order",
      "finding": "F is an arbitrary Nat-indexed family of types with a bare Norm operation for each F(k). The proof uses only real inequalities already present in hJ/hlocal, so no norm laws, continuity of J, derivative identities, target completeness, or relation between different orders is needed."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Bound on the full selected union",
      "finding": "Membership in the finite union supplies one selected local patch containing y. Its local factorial bound is followed by the scalar majorant at the same order. Thus the bound holds on the entire selected union, not merely at its centers or on K."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Empty compact set",
      "finding": "When K is empty the subtype has no elements, so the finite subcover is empty; both sums are zero and both constants are 1. The union is empty, which is a neighborhood of the empty set. The bound is vacuous without any nonempty assumption."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Conditional analytic content and computation",
      "finding": "The compact theorem explicitly assumes local bounds for every order on ambient neighborhoods. It does not establish such estimates from the Coulomb equation or continuity alone. Finite-cover selection uses Classical.choice; these existence theorems are not an algorithm for extracting computable constants or a complexity bound."
    }
  ],
